pted, get k+1 tokens per target pass. If reject at position i, output accepted prefix plus corrected token, discard rest. Speedup determined by acceptance rate, draft cost, target batch/latency, hardware, sequence length, k, parallelism, memory. Common reasons smaller: low acceptance rate due to draft mismatch/domain shift; overhead of extra target computation/memory/latency and draft cost, especially small batches or long contexts where target pass not cheap enough. Need end with two most common reasons. Maybe \"The two most common reasons...\" final sentence.\n\nNeed count words. Let's draft:\n\n\"Speculative decoding speeds up large language model inference by using a small, fast draft model to propose several future tokens before the large target model is run. The draft model generates a short sequence of candidate tokens, usually one to eight, using its own cheap autoregressive steps. The target model then processes the original prompt plus all draft tokens in a single forward pass, producing a probability distribution for each position in the sequence.\n\nVerification is done token by token. For each draft position, the target model\u2019s distribution is compared with the draft model\u2019s distribution. A rejection-sampling rule accepts the draft token when it is sufficiently likely under the target model; if it is rejected, the algorithm samples a replacement token from a corrected distribution that combines the target and draft probabilities and stops further speculation. Because the acceptance and resampling rules are derived from the", "top2": [[[1596, -0.001898301183246076], [22916, -6.763105869293213]], [[1144, -1.1920999895664863e-05], [4087, -12.136542320251465]], [[4087, -0.22429539263248444], [310, -1.6202952861785889]], [[1156, -0.0018775793723762035], [303, -6.370871543884277]], [[579, -0.2040405124425888], [25, -1.8884276151657104]], [[1622, -0.010301419533789158], [3296, -4.785087585449219]], [[25, -0.26448526978492737], [13, -1.478085517883300